@ A surface point lives only on the CPU, so it can use the computing type |simd::float3|
directly, with no loads and stores.

<<Surface points (macOS)>>=
struct SurfacePoint {
    simd::float3 pos;     // object space
    simd::float3 normal;  // unit outward geometric normal
    simd::float3 dPdu;    // direction of increasing u (tangent T, not normalized)
    simd::float3 dPdv;    // direction of increasing v (bitangent B, not normalized)
    simd::float2 uv;
};

@ A vertex, on the contrary, is copied byte for byte into a GPU buffer and must keep the
packed layout of the Direct3D version: $3+3+4+2$ floats, 48 bytes. With |simd::float3|
members it would grow to 64 bytes, with gaps, which is why the storage types of
<<Storage types for GPU data>> exist. The |static_assert| protects the layout that
<<Describe the vertex layouts to Metal>> relies on.

<<Vertex layout (macOS)>>=
// Packed like the Direct3D vertex (48 bytes); described to Metal by a vertex descriptor.
struct Vertex {
    Float3 pos;
    Float3 normal;
    Float4 tangent;  // xyz = T orthogonalized against N, w = sign so B = w * cross(N, T)
    simd::float2 uv;
};
static_assert(sizeof(Vertex) == 48);

